  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current approach relies on iterative repair and local optimization, which can easily get trapped in local optima. This new strategy employs a two-stage procedure: first, a comprehensive population-based optimization using Differential Evolution (DE) to explore diverse configurations with the integral constraint strictly enforced; second, a rigorous local refinement phase. The DE stage avoids premature convergence by using random parent sampling at each resolution level, initializing populations from non-overlapping parents. The local refinement uses a canonical gradient descent approach with an exponential barrier function to ensure integral preservation, replacing the complex sequence of optimizers from the previous solution.\n\nThis approach addresses the key bottleneck of local search by introducing multiple resolution levels and a newly designed repair mechanism that guarantees constraint satisfaction through a sorted-value adjustment method. By combining global exploration with targeted local improvement, we aim to consistently find candidates that meet or exceed the record C5 value.\n\nMathematical specification\nLet $n$ be the resolution level, typically doubled ($n=140, 280, 560, \\ldots$) when time remains. We seek probability distributions $h = (h_1, \\ldots, h_n)$ satisfying:\n\n1. $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$ for all $i$\n2. $\\sum_{i=1}^n h_i = n/2$ (ensuring $\\int_0^2 h(x) dx = 1$ with $dx = 2/n$)\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\n\\[\nC_5 = \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot \\max_{k} \\text{np.correlate}(h, 1-h, \\text{mode}=\\text{'full'})[k]\n\\]\nwhich corresponds to $\\max_k \\int_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \\cdot dx^{-1}$ scaled factors for numerical stability.\n\nThe repair function $R(h, n)$ ensures feasibility by:\n1. Clipping values to $[0,1]$\n2. Sorting the array by value order\n3. Adjusting the smallest/largest values to meet sum $=n/2$\n\nThe DE objective function is defined as:\n\\[\nf_G(h) = \\begin{cases}\n\\max_k \\text{np.correlate}(R(h, n), 1-R(h, n)) \\cdot \\frac{2}{n} & \\text{otherwise}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nImplementation procedure\nThe algorithm is divided into resolution levels. The primary flow:\n\n1. Initialization:\n   - $n$ starts at 140 based on `initial_h_values`\n   - $t_0$ = current time, $budget$ = remaining budget\n   - Parents stored in a queue $Q$: new population at each level\n\n2. For each resolution level:\n   a. Construct parent population $P_{\\text{init}} \\subset [0,1]^n$:\n      - $|P_{\\text{init}}| = 20$ points from memory at lower resolution\n      - Random bootstrap sample else\n   \n   b. Set bounds $(lb_i, ub_i) = (0,1)$ for all coordinates\n   \n   c. Perform DE optimization with:\n      - Population size $pop = \\min(40, floor(budget/5))$\n      - Strategy: DE/best1mutation with crossover factor 0.9\n      - Stop when function evaluations exceed $E_{max} = \\lfloor \\text{time\\_shared} \\times 1000 \\rfloor$\n\n   d. Repair all DE phase individuals before evaluation\n   \n   e. Update best candidate $h^*$ based on lowest $f_G$\n   \n   f. Double resolution $n \\leftarrow 2n$ for next level\n\n3. Final polishing:\n   a. Use projected gradient descent with barrier parameter $\\alpha=50$ for the best candidate\n   \n   b. Repair during each gradient step\n   \nConstraint handling\nIntegral preservation is enforced through the global repair function. When a new candidate $h$ is generated:\n1. Clip to $[0,1]$: $h_i = \\max(\\min(h_i,1),0)$\n2. Compute current integral $S = \\sum h_i$\n3. Compute adjustment: $a = (n/2 - S)/n$\n4. Sort values by index based on value magnitude\n5. Adjust smallest values first if $S < n/2$, largest if $S > n/2$\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n1. Resolution management: levels start $n=140$ doubles until computational cost meets budget\n2. Population control:\n   - Base population: 30 functions/level\n   - Max evaluations: $\\min(350, 5 \\times \\text{eval\\_time})$\n3. Terminal conditions:\n   - No improvement in $\\geq 10$ evaluations\n   - Repair fails to satisfy integral within $\\epsilon = 1e-10$\n   - Time exceeds alloted quantum: $t_{level} = \\min(300, budget \\times 0.3)$\n\nUse case variations:\n- Initialization: random uniform samples ensure grid coverage\n- Repair adjustment: dynamic scaling factor $0.01 - 0.1$ based on dimensionality\n- Gradient descent: adaptive step size $dt \\in [0.0001, 0.001]$ per coordinate\n\nComputebudgetandrobustness\nThe dominant costs are:\n- Level 1 (n=140): ~100s real-time, 80-120 evals\n- Level 2 (n=280): ~800s real-time, 50-70 evals\n- Level 3 (n=560): ~1100s real-time, 30 evals\n\nThe algorithm is structured to dynamically allocate budget reductions ($20\\% per level) and includes:\n1. Dynamic evaluation budgeting using $f_{\\text{base}} = \\text{np.median}\\{\\text{time}\\}(level)$\n2. Early termination for degenerate solutions (repair failure)\n3. Automatic fallback to lowest resolution parent\n\nValidationandreturncontract\n1. Feasibility check: $0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1$ and $\\sum h[i] \\approx n/2$ (within $1e-10$)\n2. Smoothness check: candidate DAE computed at least twice (repair + gradient)\n3. Objective check: $C_5 = \\text{np.sign}(\\min_k \\text{np.correlate})$\n4. Output format: $(h,\\min_C5,n)$\n\nThe repair function is always applied before score evaluation. The gradient bomb use energy is governed by penalty term scaling with dimensionality. Iterative budget adaption ensures we terminate optimally.",
